   "answer": 0,
   "verdict": "True",
   "explain": "<p><b>Why:</b> the count <span class=\"math\">C(n)</span> is a machine-independent number. It is the same on a phone and on a supercomputer. The running time is roughly <span class=\"math\">T(n) ≈ c<sub>op</sub>·C(n)</span>, where <span class=\"math\">c<sub>op</sub></span> is the time one basic operation takes on a particular machine, language and compiler. Counting gives you the <i>order of growth</i> (and ratios like \"doubling n quadruples the time\"). It does not give seconds. In our lab, the comparison count had constant 0.250 in both Java and Python, while the time per comparison was about 0.2 ns in Java and about 45 ns in Python.</p>\n<p><b>Why \"False\" is tempting but wrong:</b> the count and the time are <i>related</i> (proportional, as an approximation), but they are not the same thing. The count ignores the other operations, memory effects and the machine constant.</p>"
