   "verdict": "I and III",
   "explain": "<p><b>Why:</b> amortized analysis bounds the <b>total</b> cost of any sequence of m operations, then divides by m. That gives an <i>average cost per operation over the sequence</i>, guaranteed even in the worst case (Levitin §1.4 and §9.2 mention it with union–find; Tarjan formalized it). Classic example: appending to a dynamic array that doubles when full. One append can cost Θ(n) (the copy), but any n appends cost O(n) in total, so each append costs O(1) amortized.</p>\n<p><b>Why not the others:</b> II is false, and that is the whole point. Amortized analysis deliberately does <i>not</i> bound each operation individually; some operations may be expensive, as long as they are rare. So b) and d) are wrong. a) misses III, the per-operation average that the analysis produces.</p>\n<p><b>Careful:</b> \"average\" here is <b>not</b> average-case analysis. No probability is involved. It is the worst-case total divided by the number of operations.</p>"
